# Build output and vendored code: nothing here is written by hand. `public/vendor` is the
# one entry that is load-bearing today -- prettier does reformat that third-party bundle,
# which `npm run predev` then overwrites from node_modules on the next run.
dist
public/vendor

# `npm run test:coverage` writes an html report here, and prettier reformats every page of
# it. In CI the format step runs before the test step, so it would never have noticed; a
# contributor who ran coverage first would get 105 files of style complaints about a
# gitignored artifact they did not write.
coverage

# The two below are ownership boundaries, not fixes for a current misformat: checked with
# --ignore-path /dev/null, prettier leaves both byte-identical as they stand. They are listed
# so that stays a coincidence nobody has to rely on.
#
# Generated assets belong to their generator (rule 68). scripts/gen-icons.mjs emits this with
# JSON.stringify, and src/brand/appIcon.test.ts hash-checks the assets it describes; if either
# side's spacing ever moves, the formatter must not be the one that wins.
src/brand/icons.generated.json

# Weblate writes every catalog under src/locales/ but the English one, in its own JSON shape,
# and opens the pull request itself (CONTRIBUTING, "Translate it"). Listing them keeps that
# pull request from going red on a style nobody here chose. The English catalog is hand-edited
# and stays prettier's.
src/locales/*
!src/locales/en

# npm owns the lockfile's layout. Listing it keeps `npm ci` and `format:check` from ever
# having a disagreement that one of them has to lose on a dependency bump.
package-lock.json
